"proponi 3 orari per appuntamento la prossima settimana" → {"verb": "find", "object": "events"}
"suggest a free slot tomorrow morning" → {"verb": "find", "object": "events"}
"proponi 3 mattine la prossima settimana e prenotami quella che scelgo" → {"verb": "find", "object": "events"}
"suggerisci slot mercoledi e fissa il primo libero" → {"verb": "find", "object": "events"}
"propose 3 morning times next week and book one" → {"verb": "find", "object": "events"}
"suggest a free slot tomorrow and confirm" → {"verb": "find", "object": "events"}
SEMANTIC PRINCIPLE (universal, applies to ALL objects, not just events):
- Verb CREATES/SETS/WRITES/DELETES an entity (destructive/mutating action)
  → maps to the operational verb (create/set/write/delete/move/send/share).
  Examples: «fissa appuntamento» = create events, «invia mail» = send messages,
  «cancella file» = delete files, «crea cartella» = create dirs, «share
  the sheet with Y» = share files (ADR 0128 outbound consent).
- Verb ASKS/PROPOSES/SUGGESTS/SHOWS (inquiry action, read-only) → read.
  The textual synthesis of the result happens in the planner's final_answer;
  there is NO dedicated describe_<object> executor for most
  objects (exception: describe_numbers for pure numeric lists for
  statistical compute). Examples: «proponi 3 orari» = read events, «show me
  the files of X» = read files, «which ones are free?» = read events.
- If the sentence mixes both senses («if there is room book the appointment»)
  the winning intent is the MUTATING VERB (create for events post ADR 0128) —
  the check_availability is handled at planner level as the first-action
  (read_events).
NOTE ON events vs proposals:
- events = APPOINTMENTS / MEETINGS / calendar COMMITMENTS (agenda).
  "fissa/prenota/book/schedule/create appointment|meeting|encounter|meeting" = create events (ADR 0128).
- proposals = INTERNAL PROPOSALS of the Metnos synth/introvertive system
  (suggestions to improve the catalog itself). Never user-facing intent.
  If the query speaks of "appointment/meeting/encounter/meeting" it is NOT proposals.
SINGLE vs CONTAINER RULE:
When the query distinguishes between SINGLE element and CONTAINER (folder,
group, set), the object is the container if the action treats the container
as a unit (sum/count/sort BY container, work ON the
container), otherwise it is the element.
- Action on the element (read, criterion, hash, render of content,
  modification of the single one): object = element (files, messages, lines, ...).
- Action on the container (populousness, aggregated size, tree
  structure, top containers, creation/deletion of the container itself):
  object = container (dirs, ...).
"find the .py" → files (criterion on the singles).
"find the largest directories" → dirs (on the containers).
"count totals aggregating by folder" → dirs (aggregated on the container).
"total size of the path" → dirs (on the container).
"structure of the subdirectories" → dirs (on the container).
"create the folder" → dirs (action on the container itself).

Reply ONLY with the JSON, no prose.

